# Source of truth for the GitHub public mirror EXCLUDE list.
# Consumed by scripts/mirror-github.sh, scripts/trigger-github-macos.sh,
# scripts/trigger-github-build.sh, and scripts/check-mirror-exclude.mjs (guard).
#
# Format: one path per line. Everything from the first `#` to the end of the
# line is stripped as a comment — at column 0, after whitespace, or directly
# after an entry (`docs/qa  # internal checklists`). A path containing a literal
# `#` therefore cannot be expressed here. Blank lines are ignored. Leading /
# trailing whitespace is trimmed per line.
#
# Path semantics match the mirror scripts:
#   - patterns containing `/` are repo-root-relative exact paths;
#   - patterns without `/` match by basename at any depth.
#
# Adding a new internal-only document? Add it here. The guard enforces that
# the critical-paths subset (see check-mirror-exclude.mjs `CRITICAL_PATHS`)
# is always present in this file.

# GitLab CI/CD
.gitlab-ci.yml
.releaserc.json
CHANGELOG.md

# Internal scripts (GitLab registry, test infra, mirroring)
scripts/publish-packages.sh
scripts/test-mail.mjs
scripts/test-mcp-virustotal.mjs
scripts/mirror-github.sh
scripts/trigger-github-macos.sh
scripts/trigger-github-build.sh

# The export guard spells out the internal hostnames and developer paths it
# scans for, so publishing it would leak exactly what it protects. It is
# mirroring machinery like the scripts above and stays internal.
# (scripts/check-mirror-exclude.mjs and mirror-exclude.list carry no such
# markers and are published, so `npm run check:mirror-exclude` keeps working.)
scripts/check-mirror-export.mjs

# NOT excluded, on purpose: scripts/mirror-export-marker.txt. It used to be
# listed here, while its bytes were nevertheless published verbatim as
# `.mirror-export` — an entry that contradicted the one invariant this file has
# ("an excluded file's content does not reach the mirror") and framed a plainly
# public, self-describing notice as internal. The template is public data; the
# machinery that copies it (mirror-github.sh, trigger-github-build.sh,
# check-mirror-export.mjs) is what stays internal. It is allowlisted as a public
# scripts/ entry in PUBLIC_TREE_ALLOWLIST.

# Tests whose subject is itself excluded from the mirror. Keeping them would
# make `npm run test:scripts` fail in the exported tree (missing .sh /
# .claude/hooks). They stay in the internal repo, where they really do exercise
# their subject — see scripts/check-mirror-export.mjs for the export-side guard
# that keeps the remaining tooling honest.
scripts/check-mirror-exclude.test.mjs
scripts/check-mirror-export.test.mjs
scripts/trigger-github-build.test.mjs
scripts/enforce-e2e-wrapper.test.mjs
scripts/reflection-checkpoint.test.mjs
scripts/run-native-tests.test.mjs
# §2.255: the GUARD (scripts/check-release-vars.mjs) is public — a source builder
# benefits from reading why a build refuses, and it reads only variable names.
# Its test is not, for the reason above: two of its assertions read
# `.gitlab-ci.yml` (they pin that the guard is deliberately ABSENT from the two
# dispatch-only jobs), and that file is excluded a few lines up. Exported, the
# test would throw ENOENT. Making those assertions skip when the file is missing
# was rejected: they are the negative half of the wiring contract, and coverage
# that silently disappears in one environment is worse than coverage that lives
# in one repository.
scripts/check-release-vars.test.mjs

# AI agents and internal instructions
CLAUDE.md
AGENTS.md

# Internal planning / architecture documents (not user-facing)
BACKLOG.md
BACKLOG-ARCHIVE.md
docs/PRD.md
docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
docs/HEALTH_REPORT.md
docs/.health-baseline.json
# Internal manual-QA checklists (Russian, team-internal, outside docs/docs Docusaurus site)
docs/qa

# Local configs (.gitignore also blocks these, belt-and-suspenders)
.codex
.claude
.mcp.json
.env
.env.test
.env.integration
